\section{\texttt{.gitignore} is a different pattern language}

\begin{note}
The \texttt{.gitignore} file uses its own glob dialect, and it is \emph{not} the shell glob:
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{*} and \texttt{?} never cross a \texttt{/}, so each pattern is matched one path component at a time.
\item A trailing \texttt{/} restricts the pattern to directories.
\item A leading \texttt{/} anchors the pattern at the root of the repository.
\item \texttt{**} matches zero or more directories, which is the one place it does what you might expect.
\item A leading \texttt{!} negates a pattern, and the last matching pattern wins.
\item A negated pattern cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded.
\end{itemize}
Use \texttt{git check-ignore -v} to see which pattern decided that a path is ignored.
\end{note}
